      "writtenOn": "2025-02-04T23:24:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why add `const` everywhere?\n\nit doesn\u0027t matter much either way IMO, but this all went through API review, so it shouldn\u0027t be changed without a good reason\n\ni see that the rust code switched to using shared refs intead of exclusive refs. we don\u0027t necessarily want to make a promise about that in a stable ABI like this + the pointer qualifiers aren\u0027t directly connected to the rust ref semantics, they don\u0027t really need to match. `const*` is never good enough by itself to assume that taking a shared ref is OK",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 337,
        "startChar": 30,
        "endLine": 337,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "41f273cf6cb577478ece24b5d67dfba9fc453120",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "45ed4a97_87b31b52",
        "filename": "libs/libavf/include/android/virtualization.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 337,
      "author": {
        "id": 1019013
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-05T15:43:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e why add `const` everywhere?\n\nas you may expected, switching from `Box` to `Arc` enforced the change. (`Box::from_raw(*mut T)` vs `Arc::from_raw(*const T)`)\n\nmore comments are also inlined.\n\n\u003e it doesn\u0027t matter much either way IMO, but this all went through API review, so it shouldn\u0027t be changed without a good reason\n\nThis code hasn\u0027t been released officially yet, so this is the last chance to make such changes.\n\nI personally think that having `const` is more correct from the perspective of C code, especially when `const` actually works.",
      "parentUuid": "33e764ee_bd5844b1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 337,
        "startChar": 30,
        "endLine": 337,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "41f273cf6cb577478ece24b5d67dfba9fc453120",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6f763607_8455fd17",
        "filename": "libs/libavf/include/android/virtualization.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 337,
      "author": {
        "id": 1944400
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-05T18:52:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In C++ (and I assume C), `*` can be implicitly converted to `const*`, but the other direction requires an explicit cast and is generally not a good idea. By analogy, casting the `*mut T` to a `*const T` in Rust seems perfectly fine, I don\u0027t think you need to ask for a `*const T` from the caller.\n\n\u003e I personally think that having `const` is more correct from the perspective of C code, especially when `const` actually works.\n\nI suppose it is subjective, but some of these ops seem \"mutating\" to me, e.g. `start` is associating the callback with the `AVirtualMachine`.\n\n\u003e This code hasn\u0027t been released officially yet, so this is the last chance to make such changes.\n\nWe are well past the API freeze and someone people are trying to build solutions on top of it already. I\u0027d agreed its worth it if we are future proofing the API, but I think keeping it `*mut` is actually better long term because it grants us more flexibilty in the implementation (ofc, we can always ignore the qualifier, they don\u0027t have a strong meaning in C).",
      "parentUuid": "45ed4a97_87b31b52",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 337,
        "startChar": 30,
        "endLine": 337,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "41f273cf6cb577478ece24b5d67dfba9fc453120",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "876769be_4c11e4fc",
        "filename": "libs/libavf/src/lib.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 349,
