      "writtenOn": "2025-02-04T23:24:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "it isn\u0027t necessarily a problem, but i want to point out that this line won\u0027t ever actually be the one to delete the `Arc\u003cVmInstance\u003e`. the caller is holding a strong ref, so the earliest we\u0027ll actually dealloc is when the caller drops its ref\n\npersonally i think all the ref counting just so that we can pass `vm: *const AVirtualMachine` in here and it easier to call `AVirtualMachine_destroy` isn\u0027t adding much value. it is a loose style of memory management that we should be discouraging\n\nfor example, imagine if you write safe rust bindings for this API. you are going to want a rust type that owns the `vm: *const AVirtualMachine` and calls `AVirtualMachine_destroy` in its `Drop` implementation. it won\u0027t be able to make any use of this pointer argument, it will want to drop the existing instance of the rust type that owns it. if it wants to drop it from the callback, then it would do that by transferring ownership somehow, maybe via the `data` param",
      "revId": "41f273cf6cb577478ece24b5d67dfba9fc453120",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
